; link/pop_engine.link — the ENGINE memory map (P3.5).
;
; Separate from link/pop.link because the engine and the P1.x probes have
; incompatible constraints. The probes are pinned at $0200 by a harness contract
; (it reads probe_status at $0203 and gates the LOADM on the bytes at $0200); the
; engine cannot live there at all, and the reason is measured, not stylistic.
;
; WHY THE ENGINE IS NOT AT $0200 (P3.5, the whole of it):
;   Color BASIC's line-input buffer is at $02DC. LOADM places the program, and
;   then the operator types EXEC to start it -- and DECB stores those keystrokes
;   IN THE BUFFER, on top of the program that was just loaded. Caught on the bus:
;
;     f708  $02DD <- $45   'E'      from DECB, PC=$A3E7
;     f717  $02DE <- $58   'X'
;     f726  $02DF <- $45   'E'
;     f735  $02E0 <- $43   'C'
;
;   $02DD was `sta probe_phase` and $02E0 was `ldx seq_beat`. The command that
;   starts the program corrupts the program.
;
;   It is vicious because it is LAYOUT-SENSITIVE: whether it matters depends on
;   which instructions happen to sit under $02DC, so adding four bytes of
;   diagnostic changes the symptom or hides it. And it never appears on the poked
;   path (P3.2/P3.3, and the live viewer) because nothing types EXEC there --
;   which is exactly why the sequence ran correctly for Jay and "failed" under the
;   test.
;
; THE THREE REGIONS A LOADM+EXEC PROGRAM MUST CLEAR:
;   $02DC-$03D5   Color BASIC line-input buffer  <- typing EXEC lands here
;   $0400-$05FF   the text screen                <- DECB prints "OK" into it
;   $0600-$09FF   DBUF0/DBUF1/FAT/FCBs           <- LOADM itself uses these
;                 [karateka docs/project/decb-loadm-boot-gates.md; POP idioms §23]
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; CORRECTED AT P4.19. Four of the lines below had gone stale and one of them cost a
; dispatch: the region map said $0A00-$1EFF was the asset bundle and the caption save
; buffer, so a search for somewhere to put the music player concluded the intro's memory
; was full. It is not -- P3.7 moved the bundle to $3000 and P3.25 moved the save buffer to
; $5400, and NEITHER MOVE WAS REFLECTED HERE. The addresses now come from the files that
; declare them, and `harness/tools/intro_map.py` derives the whole map from the link maps
; at build time so this block can be checked rather than trusted.
;
;   $0A00-$1FFF   FREE, 5,632 B  <- was "intro asset bundle"; the bundle is at $3000
;                 (intro_seq.s BUNDLE) and the save buffer at $5400 (SAVE_BUF).
;                 The MUSIC PLAYER (section `msys`) is placed here.
;   $2000-$24FF   ENGINE (section `prog`)  <- load + exec address
;   $2500-$29A6   scene program, read from disk (-DSCENE_BASE=0x2500)
;   $3000-$52FF   intro caption bundle, 2 tracks  [intro_seq.s BUNDLE]
;   $3000-$488C   scene bundle, expanded  (-DFLAME_BASE=0x3000) -- SHARES the above;
;                 the two are never live at once and the captions are re-read after
;                 the scene [intro_seq.s, the PrincessScene note]
;   $5400-$68F1   caption save buffer  [intro_seq.s SAVE_BUF, TITLE save 5,361 B]
;   $5800-$69FF   scene packed-bundle landing zone
;   $6A00-$6A06   disk primitive parameters (-DDR_VARBASE=0x6A00, NOT $1F00)
;   $6C00-$727F   cutscene character peel buffers  [char_draw.s VIZ_PEEL/PRI_PEEL]
;   $7800-$78FF   trace ring buffer
;   $7900-$7D44   KERNEL (section `code`)
;   $7F00         stack top
;   $8000-$F7FF   MMU draw window  [gfx.s GFX_DB_WINDOW]
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; $2000 sits above every DECB region listed above. The engine's own runtime data
; stays where P3.4 put it -- also chosen to be clear of DECB, for the same reason,
; one dispatch earlier.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE LOADM CEILING IS *NOT* $25FF. MEASURED: it is in $2488..$2535.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; This file used to say the program may run up to the graphics pages' end at
; $25FF. That figure is ~174 bytes too generous and it MISLED THREE DISPATCHES
; (P3.20 twice, P3.22 once), each of which spent rounds hunting a logic bug in
; code that was fine.
;
; MEASURED at P3.22 by adding DEAD `rmb` FILLER to a known-good build -- no code,
; nothing executable, only size:
;
;     prog ending $2487   boots, suite 8/8      (the green baseline)
;     prog ending $2535   image corrupted       (baseline + 175 B of filler)
;
; so the true ceiling lies between those two, and $25FF is past it. Treat $2480 as
; the practical limit and do not spend the difference.
;
; THE SIGNATURE, so the next reader recognises it in one glance: the program's
; probe byte reads $40 (or any garbage) and the disk-read count is ZERO. That
; combination means LOADM truncated on SIZE -- DECB wrote over the image as it
; loaded -- not that the program has a bug. `$7900` reading `$FF` (kernel segment
; absent) is the same fault caught one step earlier. Jay diagnosed this on sight
; after several rounds had gone into a logic hunt.
;
; WHAT TO DO INSTEAD OF GROWING `prog`: put the code on a disk-resident track and
; reach it through a fixed table, the way the cutscene bundle does
; (link/pop_flames.link -- the cels, the blitter, char_draw and the VM all live
; there, at $4200 across two tracks). That is the established route, arrived at
; three times the hard way.

section prog load 2000
; ★ THE MUSIC PLAYER, at $0A00 (P4.19). It is 4,417 B and cannot go in `prog`: the engine
; runs to $2462 and the scene program is READ to $2500, so there are 157 bytes there. It
; cannot go in `code` either -- the kernel ends at $7D44 and the stack top is $7F00. Every
; other gap between $2000 and the trace ring totals 3,951 B across five pieces, largest
; 1,625. $0A00 takes it whole with 1,215 B to spare, above everything DECB uses.
section msys load 0E00
section code load 7900

entry probe_entry
